<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitutio Romana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">824</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitutio Romana was an agreement issued in 824 by the Frankish co-emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> with Pope Eugene II. It placed the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under joint papal and imperial supervision, created imperial envoys to oversee papal officials, and required each newly elected pope to swear loyalty to the emperor before his consecration, strengthening imperial control over papal elections.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitutio-romana.jpg" alt="Constitutio Romana" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothar_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The agreement was drawn up between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and since 817 co-emperor with his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, and Pope Eugene II, and it was confirmed on 11 November 824. Eugene's election was then being challenged by Zinzinnus, the candidate of the Roman populace, and the pope agreed to several concessions to imperial power in central Italy in return for Lothair's military and juridical support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The constitution was divided into nine articles. It declared inviolable those under the special protection of pope or emperor, required obedience to the pope and his officials, forbade the plundering of church property after a pope's death and ordered the Church's property restored. It restored the custom set by Pope Stephen III in 769 under which both clergy and laity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> took part in papal elections, and it set out to check the power of the nobles and to put down robbery with violence.</p><p>Two commissioners, one named by the pope and one by the emperor, were to report how justice was administered, so that failures could be corrected by the pope or, if he did not act, by the emperor. Each person was to be judged under the law, Roman, Salic or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a>, under which he had chosen to live. The people swore to obey the emperors Louis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>, saving their fidelity to the pope, and to allow no uncanonical election. The constitution also introduced the earliest known papal oath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Sixty years later Pope Marinus I set it aside for a time with a decree that the emperors should not interfere in the election of a pope, either directly or through their ambassadors. In 962, however, many of its clauses were reintroduced in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diploma-ottonianum">Diploma Ottonianum</a>. Modern scholars debate whether it was a dead letter with little practical impact or marked a stage on the road to imperial domination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constitutio Romana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
